<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Head to Toes! – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> All About My Body</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Head to Toes!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Locate people, objects, and things around them. Interactive: Respond to simple verbal commands in classroom settings.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can locate and identify people, objects, and things around them by describing body parts or clothing (e.g., "the boy with the blue hat" or "the red shoes"). Can follow and respond to simple commands in a game, calling out familiar body parts (e.g., "Touch your head!"). Can ask and respond to questions about familiar body parts and clothing using descriptive language (e.g., The teacher asks, "What is on your feet?" and the student responds, "These are shoes.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Simon Says" game focused on body parts and clothing. "Simon says: Touch your head!" (Teacher touches own head). "Simon says: Point to your shoes!" (Teacher points to shoes). "Simon says: Raise your hand!" (Teacher raises hand). Students follow commands using Total Physical Response (TPR). The activity emphasizes /h/ in "hand" and "head", and /t/ in "touch" and "toes".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher demonstrates the actions and repeats the target vocabulary clearly, using gestures and realia (if available, e.g., a hat, shoes). The teacher models full sentences like "This is my head." and "These are my shoes." while pointing. The teacher also models responding to "What is on your feet?" with "These are shoes."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses simple Concept Checking Questions.
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to head: "Is this my foot?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows a picture of a hat: "Can you wear this on your hand?" (Students respond: "No!") "Where does it go?" (Students: "On your head!")</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simple illustrated dialogue on a large poster or projected image, featuring two children (e.g., 'Ana' and 'Tom') identifying body parts and clothing. Picture flashcards of body parts and clothing previously introduced.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying body parts and clothing; responding to simple inquiries about them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Dialogue Example:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "Hi, Tom! What is this?" (Points to her arm)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tom: "That is your arm!"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "Yes! And what is on your head?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Tom: "This is my blue hat."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ana: "Look! These are my red shoes."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher reads the dialogue, pointing to the pictures.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Who has a hat?" (Students point to Tom).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What color are Ana's shoes?" (Students say "Red").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this a leg?" (Teacher points to an arm in the picture. Students respond: "No! It's an arm!").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice using imperative commands ("Touch your...", "Put on your..."), demonstratives ("This is...", "These are..."), possessive adjectives ("my", "your"), and WH-questions ("What is...?"). Vocabulary includes body parts (head, hand, foot, arm, leg, shoulder, knee, elbow, eye, nose, mouth, ear) and clothing (hat, shoes, shirt, pants, socks, jacket).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Choral Repetition & TPR Commands:</b> The teacher leads the class in repeating phrases and performing actions. "Touch your head!" (Class repeats and touches head). "Put on your hat!" (Class pretends to put on a hat). "This is my hand." (Class repeats and points to hand). Focus on clear pronunciation of /h/ and /t/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>"What's Missing?" Game (Semi-controlled):</b> The teacher uses flashcards of body parts and clothing. Students close their eyes. The teacher removes one card. Students open eyes and call out "What's missing?" then respond, "My [hand/hat] is missing!" or "Where is my [foot/shirt]?" Teacher guides with "Is it your [foot]?" for confirmation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>"Point and Ask" Pair Work:</b> Students work in pairs. Partner A points to a body part or article of clothing on themselves or Partner B (e.g., a knee, a shirt). Partner B says, "This is my knee" or "That is your shirt." Then they switch roles. Teacher circulates to provide support and correct pronunciation, encouraging use of "my" and "your".</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 15 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "All About My Body".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Dress-Up Buddy Description" Game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. Students work in small groups of 3-4. Each group receives a large paper doll cutout or a drawing of a person with various interchangeable clothing items (hat, shirt, pants, shoes, jacket, socks) and body parts labels to stick on.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. One student (the "describer") secretly picks a piece of clothing and puts it on the paper doll or points to a body part.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. The other students ("guessers") ask questions to identify it. E.g., "Is it a hat?" "What color is the shirt?" "Is it on the head?" or "Where is the hand?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">4. The "describer" responds using the target language: "Yes, it is a hat!" or "No, it's a jacket." "It is a blue shirt." "It's on the arm." "This is my hand."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">5. Once correctly identified, students switch roles. The teacher circulates, facilitating communication and providing support, ensuring students use demonstratives, possessive adjectives, and prepositions (on, in, under, next to).</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 10 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Dress-Up Buddy Description" game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students will actively participate in the group game, asking and answering questions about body parts and clothing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Observation Checklist):</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clearly pronounces /h/ in words like "hat," "hand," "head," and /t/ in "toes," "touch."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Uses at least 5-7 body parts (e.g., head, arm, foot) and 3-4 clothing items (e.g., hat, shoes, shirt).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Language:</b> Uses "This is/These are," "my/your," and simple WH-questions (e.g., "What is it?" "Where is it?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Actively participates by taking turns and listening to peers.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer verbally or by raising fingers (1=no, 2=a little, 3=yes).
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I say 'This is my head'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can I ask 'Where is your hat?'?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words about our body or clothes did you say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about talking about body parts and clothes?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Simon Says" warm-up was highly engaging and successfully activated prior knowledge. The "Dress-Up Buddy Description" game encouraged good interaction, and most students managed to use "This is my..." and simple questions. Some students struggled with differentiating "my" and "your" or with the pronunciation of /t/ at the end of "foot." For the next lesson, I will review possessive adjectives with clearer examples and use more specific pronunciation drills during warm-up. I will also provide picture prompts for "my" and "your" to aid visual learners.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will build on the vocabulary and structures practiced today. Students will use the names of body parts and clothing to write simple descriptive sentences about a person or a drawing, using "This is..." and "He/She is wearing..."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">[Date] <br> [Time: 5 min]</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>